{"name":"Red Inferno","hex":"#bb1e1e","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"red","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of the setting sun, blood, and strawberries; the color which is evoked by the longest visible wavelengths (between about 625–740 nm), and a primary additive color.","A revolutionary socialist or (most commonly) a Communist;  a Bolshevik, a supporter of the Bolsheviks in the Russian Civil War."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a red hue.","Having an orange-brown or orange-blond colour; ginger."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/red"},{"term":"inferno","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A place or situation resembling Hell.","A large fire; a conflagration."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/inferno"}],"entries":[{"title":"Firebrand (DC Comics)","blocks":[{"text":"Firebrand is a name of several characters appearing in American comic books published by DC Comics."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Firebrand_(DC_Comics)"},{"title":"Murat Reis the Elder","blocks":[{"text":"Murat Reis the Elder was an Ottoman privateer and admiral, who served in the Ottoman Navy. He is regarded as one of the most important Barbary corsairs."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Murat_Reis_the_Elder"},{"title":"Inferno (2016 film)","blocks":[{"text":"Inferno is a 2016 American action mystery thriller film directed by Ron Howard and written by David Koepp. It is loosely based on the 2013 novel by Dan Brown. The sequel to The Da Vinci Code (2006) and Angels & Demons (2009), it is the third and final film in the Robert Langdon film series. It stars Tom Hanks, reprising his role as Robert Langdon, alongside Felicity Jones as Dr. Sienna Brooks, Omar Sy, Sidse Babett Knudsen, Ben Foster, and Irrfan Khan."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/6/66/Inferno_%282016_film%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inferno_(2016_film)"},{"title":"Inferno (Dante)","blocks":[{"text":"Inferno is the first part of the Italian writer Dante Alighieri's 14th-century narrative poem the Divine Comedy, followed by Purgatorio and Paradiso. The Inferno describes the journey of a fictionalised version of Dante himself through Hell, guided by the ancient Roman poet Virgil. In the poem, Hell is depicted as nine concentric circles of torment located within the Earth; it is the \"realm [...] of those who have rejected spiritual values by yielding to bestial appetites or violence, or by perverting their human intellect to fraud or malice against their fellowmen\". As an allegory, the Divine Comedy represents the journey of the soul toward God, with the Inferno describing the recognition and rejection of sin."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Inferno.jpg/330px-Inferno.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inferno_(Dante)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/7/72/Firebrand1.jpg/330px-Firebrand1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/7/72/Firebrand1.jpg/330px-Firebrand1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Firebrand (Rod Reilly), cover to Police Comics #2 (September 1941).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFirebrand1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/4/42/Firebrand_%28All-Star_Squadron_no._5%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/4/42/Firebrand_%28All-Star_Squadron_no._5%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"Firebrand (Danette Reilly), from the cover to All-Star Squadron #5 (January 1982).","link":"https://en.wikipe